WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.380
Command-line arguments are input, and input deserves a trust-boundary mindset.

2
00:00:05.480 --> 00:00:15.960
A C tool may be launched by a person at a terminal, but it may also be launched by scripts, schedulers, build systems, service wrappers, package hooks, tests, or other automation.

3
00:00:16.060 --> 00:00:19.740
The source may be familiar while the actual values are still uncertain.

4
00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:23.700
Command-line values can influence program behavior quickly.

5
00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:38.360
They may select files, choose directories, set counts, control logging, enable features, change output paths, decide whether resources are created or removed, or affect memory allocation and buffer handling.

6
00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:42.380
That makes parser behavior part of the program's safety story.

7
00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:45.620
Even internal tools should parse defensively.

8
00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:48.840
Internal does not mean perfectly controlled.

9
00:00:48.940 --> 00:00:55.820
Automation changes over time, environment assumptions drift, and wrapper scripts can pass through values from another source.

10
00:00:55.920 --> 00:01:01.280
A tool that is reliable in one workflow can become fragile when reused in a larger system.

11
00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:04.120
Safe parsing starts before action.

12
00:01:04.220 --> 00:01:16.300
The parser should identify what arguments are present, what each one means, which values are required, and whether those values satisfy type, range, length, path, and format rules.

13
00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:23.760
Only after that should the program perform file changes, resource operations, memory work, or persistent state updates.

14
00:01:24.040 --> 00:01:27.720
The goal is not to make command-line interfaces complicated.

15
00:01:27.820 --> 00:01:30.220
The goal is to make them explicit.

16
00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:40.240
A reviewable parser contract tells developers what the tool accepts, what it rejects, and how it behaves when input is missing, ambiguous, or invalid.
